Runnable interface
Runnable is a predefined Java interface that contains a single method, run(). It is used to define the task that a thread should execute. To run it as a thread, we pass the Runnable object to a Thread object and then call start().
class MyThread implements Runnable {

    public void run() {

        System.out.println("Child Thread Running");

    }

}

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        MyThread obj = new MyThread();

        Thread t = new Thread(obj);

        t.start();

    }

}
Output
Child Thread Running

class Animal {

    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal is making a sound");
    }

}

class Dog extends Animal implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {

        System.out.println("Dog Thread is Running");

    }

}

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Dog d = new Dog();

        // Calling Animal class method
        d.sound();

        // Creating Thread object
        Thread t = new Thread(d);

        // Starting the thread
        t.start();

    }

}
output
Animal is making a sound
Dog Thread is Running

Synchronization
Synchronization is a process that allows only one thread to access a shared resource at a time.
It prevents data inconsistency and race conditions when multiple threads access the same object or resource.
We use Synchronization to:
Prevent data inconsistency.
Avoid race conditions.
Protect shared resources.
Ensure only one thread accesses a resource at a time.
Maintain correct program output.
program

class Table {

    synchronized void print(int n) {

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            System.out.println(n * i);
        }

    }
}

class MyThread1 extends Thread {

    Table t;

    MyThread1(Table t) {
        this.t = t;
    }

    public void run() {
        t.print(5);
    }

}

class MyThread2 extends Thread {

    Table t;

    MyThread2(Table t) {
        this.t = t;
    }

    public void run() {
        t.print(10);
    }

}

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Table obj = new Table();

        MyThread1 t1 = new MyThread1(obj);
        MyThread2 t2 = new MyThread2(obj);

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

    }

}
